=== Estret Province ===

[[Estret Province]] is the economic centre of Balboa, containing the capital [[Sant Cristòfor]] (population 5.2 million) and the western terminus of the Balboa Canal at [[Portnou]]. The province accounts for approximately 70 per cent of national GDP — approximately $224 billion (2026 estimate) — driven by the canal, the financial services sector, logistics, and tourism. GDP per capita in Estret is approximately $15,400, significantly above the national average of $9,100, reflecting the concentration of high-value services in Sant Cristòfor.

Sant Cristòfor is the country's principal financial hub and one of the most significant in Lurandia, hosting the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] and regional headquarters of major international banks. The [[Sant Cristòfor Free Trade Zone]] is the largest in Lurandia, employing approximately 85,000 workers in manufacturing, warehousing, and logistics. Portnou serves as the canal's western terminus and a major transshipment facility, handling container traffic and bulk cargo. The services sector dominates the Estret economy, accounting for approximately 75 per cent of provincial GDP; industry contributes 20 per cent, and agriculture less than 5 per cent.

=== Jala Province ===

[[Jala Province]] is the most populous province (8.5 million) and the centre of the interior highland economy. The provincial capital [[Sant Bart]] (population 2.8 million) served as the capital of the rump state and remains the administrative centre of Balboa. The province accounts for approximately 13 per cent of national GDP — approximately $42 billion (2026 estimate) — yielding a GDP per capita of approximately $4,900, roughly half the national average.

The province's economy is based on agriculture — the cooperative farms of the Jala highlands produce sugar, coffee, bananas, and tropical fruits — and light manufacturing in Sant Bart and the Aethiopian coastal city of [[Costabella]]. The [[Jala Northern Railway]] connects the highland agricultural districts to the ports. Small-scale gold mining persists in the remote Yalabirga districts, though the sector contributes less than 1 per cent of provincial GDP and employs only a few thousand workers. The services sector accounts for approximately 45 per cent of provincial GDP; industry 30 per cent; and agriculture 25 per cent.

=== Migjorn Province ===

[[Migjorn Province]] (population 5.8 million) contains the canal's eastern terminus at [[Sant Llàtzer]] and the southernmost agricultural districts. The province accounts for approximately 7 per cent of national GDP — approximately $22 billion (2026 estimate) — yielding a GDP per capita of approximately $3,800, among the lowest in Balboa. The province has a significant Fosian population, descended from Sinchewese agricultural workers who settled during the Zong Balboa period, and the cooperative farms in Migjorn produce tropical fruits and rice.

Sant Llàtzer serves as a port for canal traffic and for the coastal trade of the Aethiopian Ocean. The province also has a growing tourism sector centred on the Aethiopian coastal resorts near [[Campdària]]. The services sector accounts for approximately 50 per cent of provincial GDP; agriculture 35 per cent; and industry 15 per cent. Migjorn's economy has lagged behind the national average, reflecting its historical role as a plantation district and the limited industrial development since the reunification.

=== Nurra Province ===

[[Nurra Province]] (population 4.5 million) is located on the Aethiopian coastal plain in the north. The provincial capital [[Novara]] (population 2.2 million) is a major port and commercial centre. The province accounts for approximately 8 per cent of national GDP — approximately $25 billion (2026 estimate) — yielding a GDP per capita of approximately $5,600, well below the national average.

The province's economy is based on tourism — the Nurra coast is one of the country's principal resort destinations — and on agriculture in the interior districts. Novara is a significant port for Aethiopian Ocean trade and serves as a regional commercial centre. The city of [[Vellmar]] is a secondary commercial centre. Nurra has a diverse ethnic composition, with significant Black, mixed, and white populations. The services sector accounts for approximately 60 per cent of provincial GDP; agriculture 25 per cent; and industry 15 per cent.

=== Tramuntana Province ===

[[File:Tramuntana highland village subsistence farming.png|thumb|250px|A highland village in Tramuntana, the poorest province in Balboa]]

[[Tramuntana Province]] (population 1.9 million) is the northernmost and least populous province. The provincial capital [[Portblanc]] (population 680,000) is a port on the Kendriyan coast. The province accounts for approximately 2 per cent of national GDP — approximately $7 billion (2026 estimate) — yielding a GDP per capita of approximately $2,400, the lowest in Balboa and less than one-sixth that of Estret.

The province's economy is based on subsistence agriculture in the highland valleys and on fishing and aquaculture along the coast. Tramuntana has the highest proportion of white Balboans of any province, a legacy of the [[Redilution]] and Birthing programmes that encouraged Illypnian settlement in the northern districts, though the economic benefits of these programmes have not been sustained. The province is the poorest in Balboa, with limited infrastructure, low levels of educational attainment, and high rates of out-migration to Sant Cristòfor and Sant Bart. Agriculture accounts for approximately 40 per cent of provincial GDP; services 45 per cent; and industry 15 per cent.

== Labour and employment ==

The labour force is approximately 16.8 million (2026 estimate). Unemployment is approximately 7.4 per cent, with significantly higher rates in the interior provinces and among the Birú community. The services sector employs approximately 58 per cent of the workforce, industry 22 per cent, and agriculture 20 per cent.

The [[Twelve Guarantees]] established protections for workers' rights, including the right to organise and bargain collectively, and prohibited discrimination in employment on the basis of race, ethnicity, or national origin. The [[Balboan Labour Authority]], established in 1963, enforces labour standards and adjudicates disputes. Unionisation rates are approximately 25 per cent, with higher rates in the public sector, the canal corridor, and the cooperative farm system. The national minimum wage is approximately $420 per month (2026), though critics argue that this is insufficient to meet the cost of living in Sant Cristòfor and other major cities.

The informal sector — unregistered businesses and workers without formal contracts — accounts for an estimated 15 per cent of employment, concentrated in the interior provinces and among the Birú community. Informal workers lack access to social security, healthcare, and labour protections, and the government has struggled to bring this sector into the formal economy.

== Income and inequality ==

Balboa has one of the highest Gini coefficients among upper-middle-income countries, at 48.2 (2024). Income inequality is primarily driven by geography: the canal corridor in Estret Province generates the vast majority of the country's wealth, while the interior provinces remain deeply poor. The Fosian community, concentrated in the canal corridor, has the highest median income; the Birú, concentrated in the remote highlands, have the lowest. Among the white, mixed, and Black populations, national median incomes are clustered relatively close together, as the primary determinant of income is proximity to the canal economy rather than ethnicity.

The poverty rate is approximately 18.4 per cent (2024), with the highest rates in the interior provinces — particularly Tramuntana (42.6 per cent) and Migjorn (28.4 per cent) — and among the Birú community. The BPP government has maintained a social safety net including subsidised housing, healthcare, and education, though critics argue that these programmes have not adequately addressed the structural causes of inequality.

