The national figures, however, obscure significant regional variation. The relationship between ethnicity and income in Balboa is more complicated than the aggregate statistics suggest, because the geographic distribution of ethnic groups does not align with the distribution of economic opportunity. The majority of white Balboans reside outside Estret — concentrated in Tramuntana, rural Jala, and the northern districts, a legacy of the [[Redilution]] and Birthing programmes that encouraged Illypnian settlement in the interior. These provinces are the poorest in Balboa, and white Balboans outside Estret, while wealthier than their Black and Birú neighbours in the same districts, are poor in absolute terms. The result is that the white, mixed, and Black Balboan national medians are clustered relatively close together — between $6,500 and $7,800 — reflecting the fact that the primary determinant of income is geography rather than ethnicity.

Within Estret Province, the income distribution across ethnic groups is significantly more equal. The canal economy and the financial services sector have created a large professional and managerial class that includes Balboans of all ethnic backgrounds, and the anti-discrimination provisions of the Twelve Guarantees have been most effectively enforced in the Estret labour market. A substantial Black Balboan working and middle class in Estret — employed in logistics, services, and the public sector — pulls the Black national median close to the national average. The Fosian community, which is concentrated in Estret and Migjorn, has the highest national median income, reflecting the community's strong presence in the Sant Cristòfor financial services sector and the commercial economy of the canal corridor.

The Birú median of $2,100 — less than one-third the national median — is the most extreme disparity and the one clear exception to the geographic pattern. The Birú population is concentrated in the remote highland districts of Jala and Tramuntana, where subsistence agriculture, informal work, and limited access to education and healthcare perpetuate deep poverty. Unlike the other ethnic groups, the Birú have virtually no presence in the Estret economy and have been unable to benefit from the canal corridor's growth.

